  "response": "The most likely cause is `direct_date` in `src/billing.ts`: seven separate wall-clock reads at lines 57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, and 273.\n\nIf `generateInvoice` crosses midnight—or fake timers advance between calls—different parts of one invoice can observe different dates. That fits intermittent date-boundary failures. The 204-line `generateInvoice` makes this temporal coupling harder to notice.\n\nProposed fix:\n\n```ts\ntype Clock = {\n  now(): Date;\n};\n\nconst systemClock: Clock = {\n  now: () => new Date(),\n};\n\nexport function generateInvoice(input: InvoiceInput, clock = systemClock) {\n  const now = clock.now(); // one consistent instant per operation\n\n  // Pass `now` into extracted calculation helpers.\n}\n```\n\nThen replace every `Date.now()`/`new Date()` used by that operation with the captured `now` or `now.getTime()`.\n\nAdd deterministic tests with a fixed clock for:\n\n- Immediately before and after midnight.\n- Month/year rollover.\n- The production timezone and UTC.\n- A DST transition, if billing periods are calendar-day based.\n\nA secondary suspect is `src/date.ts`, which mixes UTC and local methods and parses timezone-less strings. If billing calls those helpers, standardize calculations on UTC and require explicit timezone markers. The weak billing tests should also be strengthened to assert exact invoice dates and period boundaries, not merely that an invoice exists.",
